  <span class="os-title-label">Figure </span>
<span class="os-number">6.4</span>



  <span class="os-divider"> </span>
  <span class="os-caption">The spectrum of radiation emitted from a quartz surface (blue curve) and the blackbody radiation curve (black curve) at 600 K.</span>
</figcaption></figure></div>

<p id="fs-id1163713275775">Two important laws summarize the experimental findings of blackbody radiation: <em data-effect="italics">Wien’s displacement law</em> and <em data-effect="italics">Stefan’s law</em>. <span data-type="term" class="no-emphasis" id="term-00006" group-by="W">Wien’s displacement law</span> is illustrated in <a href="6-1-blackbody-radiation#CNX_UPhysics_39_01_BBradcurve" class="autogenerated-content" aria-label="View Figure 6.3">Figure 6.3</a> by the curve connecting the maxima on the intensity curves. In these curves, we see that the hotter the body, the shorter the wavelength corresponding to the emission peak in the radiation curve. Quantitatively, Wien’s law reads</p>

